### Why `build:libs` comes first

Apps import libraries from `./dist/<lib>` — `tsconfig.json` maps each
library's name to its build output — not from source, and `ng serve` won't
build one on its own. So the first thing a fresh clone needs is:

```bash
npm run build:libs
```

Run it again any time a library's public API changes. Every `npm` script that
needs it runs it for you first: `npm start`, `npm run watch`, `npm run build`,
`npm test` and `npm run e2e`, whether from the root or with `-w`. Only `ng serve` and
`ng test` invoked directly skip it, so those are the two that need it by hand.

Working on a library and an app that consumes it at the same time? Run this
in the background to rebuild the library on every save:

```bash
npm run watch:libs
```

### Theming

Every app's `styles.scss` pulls in the design system's tokens, so every app
gets light and dark for free — a visitor's `prefers-color-scheme` decides,
in CSS, with nothing to wire up.

The stylesheet also declares every palette in both modes, keyed off two
attributes on `<html>`:

| Attribute | Values | Effect |
| --------- | ------ | ------ |
| `data-theme` | `light`, `dark`, or absent | Forces a colour scheme. Absent means "follow the OS." |
| `data-palette` | A palette name | Picks a palette. Absent means `default`. |

Whatever sets them gets every combination for free — nothing re-renders, and
no component needs to know theming exists. Storybook's toolbar sets them for
the stories, and a fixed `data-palette` on `<html>` in an app's `index.html`
picks that app's brand. To let visitors choose, the `theming` schematic puts a
toggle in every app's header (see "Adding to this workspace").

To add a palette:

1. Declare it in `<%= libRoot %>/src/styles/_ref.scss`, and in the palette
   toolbar in `<%= libRoot %>/.storybook/preview.ts`.
2. Check it against WCAG, in light and dark:

   ```bash
   npm run check:contrast
   ```
