## Adding to this workspace

The generator stays installed, so the same schematics that built this
workspace also extend it:

```bash
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:app <name>          # a client-rendered app
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:marketing <name>    # a prerendered static site
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:ui-lib <name>       # a design-system library
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:theming             # a theme toggle in every app's header
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:mobile <app>        # a Capacitor shell for an app
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:codegen             # an OpenAPI client
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:i18n --locales en,fr  # runtime translation
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:packages cdk        # a curated package: cdk, aria, …
```

Each one wires itself into the workspace it finds: new scripts land in the
table below, and anything that needs explaining is written into this file.
Theme switching, translation and the curated packages also reach every app or
site you generate after them, so the order you add things in does not matter.

## Dependency policy

This workspace passed a security audit the day it was generated, and it ships
with the tools to keep passing.

```bash
npm run audit:policy          # resolve the lockfile, audit it, fail on anything new
npm run doctor                # show how you've drifted from the generator's current policy
npx angular-capacitor-workspace doctor --fix   # apply the fix
```

`package.json` carries two pieces of that policy:

- **`overrides`** — pins on transitive dependencies that fix known
  vulnerabilities with no upstream fix yet. Each one is explained in the
  generator's policy module.
- **`allowScripts`** — an allowlist (npm ≥ <%= npmFloor %>) for which dependencies may
  run install scripts. CI runs with `--strict-allow-scripts`, so a new
  dependency that wants to run one will fail the build until someone
  approves it.

To pick up newer fixes:

```bash
ng update angular-capacitor-workspace@<%= angularLine %>
npx angular-capacitor-workspace doctor --fix
```

`ng update` also runs the generator's migrations, which fix files it wrote here.
A file you have edited is left as it is, and the change to make by hand is
printed instead. A plain `npm install` runs no migrations;
`ng update angular-capacitor-workspace --migrate-only --from=<old version>`
runs them afterwards.

## Scripts

The root `package.json` holds the scripts that concern the whole workspace,
and names no project. Each app, site and mobile shell keeps its own in a
`package.json` of its own — `projects/<app>/web` and `projects/<app>/mobile` —
registered as an npm workspace member. From the root, `npm start`, `watch`,
`build`, `test` and `e2e` take the project's name (`npm run build shop`), and
`build`, `test` and `e2e` without one run every project. Anything else in a
project's `package.json` runs with `-w <package>`. Every
time you generate something new with `angular-capacitor-workspace`, it adds a
row here for the scripts it created, so this table always reflects what the
workspace can actually run. Rows you edit by hand are left alone.

<!-- angular-capacitor-workspace:scripts -->

| Script | What it does |
| ------ | ------------ |
| `npm run audit:policy` | runs the same dependency check that ran when this workspace was created |
| `npm run doctor` | shows how this workspace has drifted from the current dependency policy |

